<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Sources and further reading</h2><ul class="essay-refs"><li>Mann, C. C. (2005) 1491: New Revelations of the Americas Before Columbus. New York: Alfred A. Knopf.</li><li>Morgan, L. H. (1870) Systems of Consanguinity and Affinity of the Human Family.</li><li>Morgan, L. H. (1904) League of the Ho-dé-no-sau-nee or Iroquois. New York: Dodd, Mead.</li><li>Parker, A. C. (1916) The Constitution of the Five Nations. Albany: The University of the State of New York.</li><li>Taylor, A. (2006) The Divided Ground: Indians, Settlers, and the Northern Borderland of the American Revolution.</li><li>Wallace, P. A. W. (1946) The Iroquois Book of Life: White Roots of Peace.</li><li>Williams, K. P. (2018) Kayanerenkó:wa: The Great Law of Peace. Winnipeg: University of Manitoba Press.</li></ul><p class="soc-method">The shares are of the whole population, children included. Where a scholar has counted or estimated a rank, its share follows that work, named on the rank; where no count survives, the share is an estimate, marked est. and drawn dashed, made from what the rank was and did, from related figures and from the same rank in comparable societies, and its note says what it rests on. Population: Estimate: modern estimates for the 17th century range from 5,500 to more than 100,000; Snow and Jones put the people at about 20,000 before the epidemics of the 1630s, Jones at 8,000 for 1634–1660 without the thousands adopted from conquered peoples, and the contemporary Lahontan at 70,000 around 1690.</p></section>
